End-to-end evidence for the cross-language consumer fix in bin/fm-extension.mjs
(releaseLifecycleLock), isolated by running just the affected conformance
section (FM_EXTENSION_BINDING_SEGMENT=lifecycle-flow) of the real
tests/fm-extension-binding.test.sh suite against three points in this branch's
history.

1) Intermediate commit 31e46e70948355ff8a830446522d5b637b8a5ec0 (has the
   shell-side pid-identity recording, does NOT yet have the fm-extension.mjs
   release fix) - reproduces the exact bug the intent describes:

   $ FM_EXTENSION_BINDING_SEGMENT=lifecycle-flow bash tests/fm-extension-binding.test.sh
   error[binding-failed]: error[internal]: ENOTEMPTY: directory not empty, rmdir
     '.../.extension-binding-lifecycle.lock.owner.50qsdl'
   not ok - local bind returned no binding retirement identity
   EXIT:1

2) Target commit 68238561bc13fb4fa2fd1d98deeb3b31947717bc (this change, with the
   fm-extension.mjs fix that clears every file the owner directory actually
   holds instead of a hardcoded "pid" unlink) - the same section passes cleanly:

   $ FM_EXTENSION_BINDING_SEGMENT=lifecycle-flow bash tests/fm-extension-binding.test.sh
   ok - one external adapter registers, invokes, captures unhandled evidence, classifies, and terminally retires end to end
   ok - inner result.silent host crash releases the parent lifecycle lock before terminal retry
   ok - local binding retirement requires its exact identity and disables invocation

   all extension-binding tests passed
   EXIT:0

This directly demonstrates: the pid-identity recording introduced a second file
into a lock's owner directory; the pre-fix JS release logic (unlink "pid" only)
left that file behind and the rmdir failed ENOTEMPTY; the shipped fix (read the
owner directory and clear whatever is actually there) resolves it end-to-end
through the real bash-acquires/JS-releases lifecycle path.
